<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=binary-number">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Binary number</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1679">1679</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as binary numeral system, base-2</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A binary number is a number expressed in the base-2 numeral system, which uses only the symbols 0 and 1 and is a positional notation with a radix of 2. The modern system was first studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the 15th, 16th and 17th centuries by Thomas Harriot, whose results were unpublished, and decades later by Gottfried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Leibniz</a>, who is credited for the invention. Leibniz's first known dedicated work, On the Binary Progression, dates from 1679, and his paper on binary arithmetic was published in 1703. Related systems appear earlier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Almost all modern computers use the binary system.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Europe, Leibniz wrote the first commentary in 1703, arguing that the book showed the universality of <b>binary numbers</b>, while Hegel criticised the binary system and Chinese characters as &quot;empty forms&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/work/i-ching">I Ching · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1679</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz</a> · Thomas Harriot</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Egypt (Horus-Eye fractions) (c. 2400 BCE); India (Pingala's metres) (c. 2nd century BCE)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Binary-like systems appear in ancient Egypt (Horus-Eye fractions, early forms c. 2400 BCE), in the Chinese I Ching, in Pingala's description of metres in India (c. 2nd century BCE) and in Ifá divination. Thomas Harriot investigated binary before Leibniz but did not publish.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-arabic-numeral-system">Hindu–Arabic numerals<span>1st–4th century CE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=binary-number">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
